\section{Where the sharp bound lives}

\Cref{thm:half} is not the extremal answer. The complete intersection theorem
for $t$-intersecting families --- established for the 1-intersecting case by
Wilson \cite{Wilson1984} following Erd\H{o}s, Ko and Rado's original theorem
\cite{EKR1961}, and for general $t$ by Ahlswede and Khachatrian
\cite{AhlswedeKhachatrian1997} --- gives the exact maximum for every
$t$-intersecting family of $k$-subsets of an $m$-set, as a function of $m$,
$k$, and $t$. Specialized to $t=2$, $k=2n$, $m=4n$, that theory produces a
bound strictly below $\tfrac12\binom{4n}{2n}$, by a correction term of order
$\binom{2n}{n}^2$: the extremal families are not the complement-avoiding
families this pairing argument bounds, but a sharper construction that the
pairing argument, by design, cannot see. \Cref{thm:half} is the bound the
complement involution alone supplies, and it is exactly as strong as that one
idea can make it.

\section{Verification}

\Cref{thm:half} is formalized in \Lean{} against Mathlib as an injection
$F \hookrightarrow U(n)\smallsetminus F$ built from the complement involution,
depending on \texttt{propext}, \texttt{Classical.choice}, and
\texttt{Quot.sound} only.

