29	\begin{document}
30	
31	\begin{center}
32	{\LARGE\bfseries UNO Reverse}
33	\end{center}
34	\vspace{1em}
35	
36	\begin{abstract}
37	This paper isolates a structural relation between classical two-valued semantics and the four-valued paraconsistent semantics associated with Belnap--Dunn logic. We show that the Boolean algebra $\mathbf{2}=\{F,T\}$ occurs as a proper semantic core $\mathbf{B}_4=\{F,T\}\subsetneq\mathbf{4}$ inside Belnap FOUR, while the additional values $N$ and $B$ represent, respectively, informational incompleteness and inconsistency. Ordered by the truth lattice, the inclusion of the Boolean core has distinct left and right adjoints: the reflector sends $N,B$ to $T$, while the coreflector sends $N,B$ to $F$. These constructions lift coordinate-wise to the product representation of the sixteen-valued trilattice, yielding a four-element Boolean core inside the sixteen-valued ambient.
38	
39	We then consider the constant information-order closure $\operatorname{Inc}(v)=B$. Since $B$ is a fixed point outside the Boolean core, this operation cannot factor through the Boolean inclusion; the same obstruction persists under the sixteen-valued lift. The structural conclusion is one of containment and retraction. Classical two-valued semantics is not coextensive with the ambient and does not stand beside it as an equally exhaustive semantic universe. It is obtained by selecting the proper subspace $\mathbf B_4=\{F,T\}$ and retracting ambient values back into that image. The surrounding values $N$ and $B$ are states of the ambient that the classical construction excludes by design. In this precise sense, the Boolean world is a constructed semantic artifice entirely internal to, and strictly smaller than, the paraconsistent sea. The closure $\operatorname{Inc}(v)=B$ exposes the boundary: it stabilizes a point that the Boolean retract cannot contain. The detonation of \textit{ex falso} is thereby absorbed by an ambient in which contradiction remains a legitimate semantic state.
40	\end{abstract}
41	
42	%──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
43	\section{Introduction: From Boolean Determinacy to Paraconsistent Information}
44	
45	The two-valued structures now associated with Boolean logic have their algebraic origin in the work of George Boole, whose mid-nineteenth-century programme connected logical reasoning with algebraic operations \cite{Boole1854}. The modern theory of Boolean algebras subsequently provided a standard algebraic semantics for propositional reasoning, while classical propositional logic became canonically associated with valuations into the two-element algebra
46	\[
47	\mathbf{2}=\{F,T\}
48	\]
49	In this setting, every proposition receives exactly one of two truth values.
50	
51	That restriction is historically and mathematically powerful, but it is not the only possible semantic architecture. In the twentieth century, alternative logics were developed to handle informational incompleteness, inconsistency, indeterminacy, and other departures from classical assumptions. One particularly influential construction is Belnap's four-valued semantics, developed in connection with the problem of reasoning from inconsistent and incomplete information and closely associated with the work of Dunn on relevant and paraconsistent semantics \cite{Belnap1977,Dunn1976}.
52	
53	The resulting four-valued structure distinguishes not only truth and falsity but also the informational possibilities of having neither truth nor falsity and of having both. This distinction became foundational for later work on bilattices, beginning with the systematic algebraic treatment of Ginsberg and subsequently developed by Fitting and others \cite{Ginsberg1988,Fitting1991,Fitting2002}. The central question of the present paper is therefore structural: how, exactly, is the classical two-valued universe carved out of the richer paraconsistent space? The answer developed below is not merely that $\mathbf2$ embeds in $\mathbf4$. The inclusion participates in retractions: the ambient can be collapsed back onto the Boolean image while remaining strictly larger than that image. Classicality is therefore represented as an internally constructed restriction of the ambient rather than as an exhaustive description of the ambient itself.
54	
55	%──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
56	\section{The Paraconsistent Ambient: Belnap FOUR}
57	
58	Belnap's FOUR was introduced as a semantic structure capable of representing information that may be incomplete or inconsistent \cite{Belnap1977}. Its subsequent role in the development of bilattice theory made explicit the importance of distinguishing truth order from information order \cite{Ginsberg1988,Fitting1991}.
59	\begin{definition}[Belnap FOUR]
60	Let
61	\[
62	\mathbf{4}=\{N,T,F,B\}
63	\]
64	be Belnap's four-valued algebra with negation
65	\[
66	\neg N=N,\qquad
67	\neg T=F,\qquad
68	\neg F=T,\qquad
69	\neg B=B
70	\]
71	The intended readings are:
72	\[
73	N=\text{Neither},\qquad
74	T=\text{True},\qquad
75	F=\text{False},\qquad
76	B=\text{Both}
77	\]
78	FOUR carries an information order $\le_i$, a truth order $\le_t$, and the corresponding dual falsity order \cite{Belnap1977,Fitting1991}. The truth order is
79	\[
80	F<_tN<_tT,\qquad
81	F<_tB<_tT
82	\]
83	with $N$ and $B$ incomparable.
84	The information order is
85	\[
86	N<_iT<_iB,\qquad
87	N<_iF<_iB
88	\]
89	with $T$ and $F$ incomparable.
90	The falsity order is the dual ordering
91	\[
92	T<_fN<_fF,\qquad
93	T<_fB<_fF
94	\]
95	
96	\begin{figure}[htbp]
97	\centering
98	\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.88]
99	  % Truth order
100	  \begin{scope}[xshift=-5.2cm]
101	    \node[font=\bfseries] at (0,2.95) {Truth order $\le_t$};
102	    \node[fournode] (tT) at (0,2.15) {$T$};
103	    \node[fournode] (tN) at (-1.15,1.05) {$N$};
104	    \node[fournode] (tB) at (1.15,1.05) {$B$};
105	    \node[fournode] (tF) at (0,-0.05) {$F$};
106	    \draw[thick] (tF)--(tN)--(tT);
107	    \draw[thick] (tF)--(tB)--(tT);
108	  \end{scope}
109	  % Information order
