38	\begin{abstract}
39	This paper isolates a structural relation between classical two-valued semantics and the four-valued paraconsistent semantics associated with Belnap--Dunn logic. We show that the Boolean algebra $\mathbf{2}=\{F,T\}$ occurs as a proper semantic core $\mathbf{B}_4=\{F,T\}\subsetneq\mathbf{4}$ inside Belnap FOUR, while the additional values $N$ and $B$ represent, respectively, informational incompleteness and inconsistency. Ordered by the truth lattice, the inclusion of the Boolean core has distinct left and right adjoints: the reflector sends $N,B$ to $T$, while the coreflector sends $N,B$ to $F$. These constructions lift coordinate-wise to the product representation of the sixteen-valued trilattice, yielding a four-element Boolean core inside the sixteen-valued ambient.
40	
41	We then consider the constant information-order closure $\operatorname{Inc}(v)=B$. Since $B$ is a fixed point outside the Boolean core, this operation cannot factor through the Boolean inclusion; the same obstruction persists under the sixteen-valued lift. The structural conclusion is one of containment and retraction. Classical two-valued semantics is not coextensive with the ambient and does not stand beside it as an equally exhaustive semantic universe. It is obtained by selecting the proper subspace $\mathbf B_4=\{F,T\}$ and retracting ambient values back into that image. The surrounding values $N$ and $B$ are states of the ambient that the classical construction excludes by design. In this precise sense, the Boolean world is a constructed semantic artifice entirely internal to, and strictly smaller than, the paraconsistent sea. The closure $\operatorname{Inc}(v)=B$ exposes the boundary: it stabilizes a point that the Boolean retract cannot contain. The detonation of \textit{ex falso} is thereby absorbed by an ambient in which contradiction remains a legitimate semantic state.
42	\end{abstract}
