55	\begin{abstract}
56	A finite set $A \subset \mathbb{N}$ has \emph{distinct subset sums} if no two of
57	its subsets share a sum. We give the elementary proof that such a set satisfies
58	$\sum_{a \in A} a \ge 2^{|A|} - 1$, hence $\sum_{a \in A} a \ge \tfrac12 \cdot
59	2^{|A|}$: the subset-sum map is injective on the $2^{|A|}$ subsets of $A$, and
60	every value it takes lies between $0$ and $\sum_{a \in A} a$. The bound is tight,
61	achieved by the powers of two. It is also, by a wide margin, not the bound Erd\H{o}s
62	conjectured. This paper proves the counting bound and states precisely where it
63	stops.
64	\end{abstract}
65	
66	\section{Distinct subset sums}
67	
68	\begin{definition}
69	A finite set $A \subset \mathbb{N}$ has \emph{distinct subset sums} if the map
70	$S \mapsto \sum_{a \in S} a$ is injective on $\mathcal{P}(A)$, the power set of
71	$A$: whenever $S, T \subseteq A$ and $\sum_{a \in S} a = \sum_{a \in T} a$, then
72	$S = T$.
73	\end{definition}
74	
75	The powers of two, $A = \{1, 2, 4, \dots, 2^{n-1}\}$, are the standard example:
76	every subset sum is a distinct binary numeral. Erd\H{o}s asked, in 1931, how
77	small the largest element of such a set can be forced to be
78	\cite{ErdosProblem1}. The question is still open, carries a \$500 prize, and is
79	usually stated as the conjecture that $\max(A) \ge c \cdot 2^n / \sqrt{n}$ for
80	an absolute constant $c$ --- a factor of $\sqrt{n}$ below what the powers of two
81	give. What follows is not that theorem. It is the counting argument that any
82	solution to the problem sits above, proved in full and with nothing hidden past
83	it.
84	
85	\section{The injection}
86	
87	\begin{theorem}[Subset-sum injection bound]
88	\label{thm:main}
89	If $A \subset \mathbb{N}$ is finite and has distinct subset sums, then
90	\[
91	  \boxed{\; \sum_{a \in A} a \;\ge\; 2^{|A|} - 1 . \;}
92	\]
93	\end{theorem}
94	
95	\begin{proof}
96	Every $a \in A$ satisfies $a \ge 1$: if some $a = 0$ then $\{a\}$ and $\varnothing$
97	would have the same sum, contradicting distinctness. Write $\Sigma = \sum_{a \in
98	A} a$.
99	
100	The subset-sum map $f : \mathcal{P}(A) \to \mathbb{N}$, $f(S) = \sum_{a \in S} a$,
101	is injective by hypothesis, and its image lies in $\{0, 1, \dots, \Sigma\}$: every
102	subset sum is nonnegative and at most the sum of all of $A$. An injection from a
103	set of size $2^{|A|}$ into a set of size $\Sigma + 1$ forces
104	\[
105	  2^{|A|} \;\le\; \Sigma + 1,
106	\]
107	which rearranges to the claim.
108	\end{proof}
109	
110	\begin{figure}[h]
111	\centering
112	\begin{tikzpicture}[
113	  box/.style={draw, thick, rounded corners, minimum width=3.6cm, minimum height=1.6cm, align=center},
114	  arr/.style={-{Latex[length=2.5mm]}, thick},
115	]
