\begin{document}
\title{\textbf{Three Erdős Problems and the Boundary of Witnessed Closure}}
\author{C.\ Lando Mills\\
Trabuco Canyon, California\\
\texttt{c.landonmills@gmail.com}\\
ORCID: \href{https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0003-0552}{0000-0003-0003-0552}}
\date{31 August 2026}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A large corpus of Erd\H{o}s's open problems has been formalized and checked
against a common standard: an explicit, checked witness together with a
closure that survives compilation, not a claim carried only in a comment.
Exactly three problems in that corpus meet the standard --- distinct subset
sums, two-intersecting families, and Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz --- and we
give all three proofs here, elementary, complete, and unconditional. The
point of collecting them is not merely economy. Each proof is the entire
closure a witnessed, constructive method reaches for its problem, and each
problem's actual open question --- Erd\H{o}s's sharp asymptotic constant, the
extremal Frankl--F\"uredi bound, the general Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz
theorem for every $n$ --- sits past that closure, reachable only by
apparatus this method does not use. We say plainly what that apparatus is
and why invoking it is not a way of seeing further into the same object.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} distinct subset sums \(\cdot\) two-intersecting
families \(\cdot\) Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz \(\cdot\) witness and closure
\(\cdot\) constructive proof \(\cdot\) Lean 4 \(\cdot\) axiom of choice
\(\cdot\) propositional extensionality \(\cdot\) quotient soundness

\section{The census}

Recall a distinction that matters for a formalized proof and is invisible in
an informal one. A \emph{witness} is an explicit, checked object standing
behind a claim: a construction the kernel can run, not a name attached to an
assertion. A \emph{closure} is a verdict that survives compilation on that
witness, not a comment claiming one. Proofs written this way are not
automatically weaker than proofs that reach for classical machinery; they
state exactly what they used to close, and what they did not.

A corpus of Erd\H{o}s's problems was formalized in \Lean{} against this
standard, header by header. Checking each file by building it, not by
reading its comment, finds exactly three problems that carry a real witness
and a real closure end to end: Erd\H{o}s's question on sets with distinct
subset sums, his question on families of sets intersecting pairwise in at
least two points, and Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz. \Cref{sec:sums,sec:two-int,sec:efl}
give all three in full. Nothing here is an excerpt or a
summary; each section is a complete, standalone proof, the same content
that stood as its own manuscript before being collected here.

\section{Distinct subset sums}
\label{sec:sums}

\begin{definition}
A finite set $A \subset \mathbb{N}$ has \emph{distinct subset sums} if the map
$S \mapsto \sum_{a \in S} a$ is injective on $\mathcal{P}(A)$, the power set of
$A$: whenever $S, T \subseteq A$ and $\sum_{a \in S} a = \sum_{a \in T} a$, then
$S = T$.
\end{definition}

The powers of two, $A = \{1, 2, 4, \dots, 2^{n-1}\}$, are the standard example:
every subset sum is a distinct binary numeral. Erd\H{o}s asked, in 1931, how
small the largest element of such a set can be forced to be
\cite{ErdosProblem1}. The question is still open, carries a \$500 prize, and is
usually stated as the conjecture that $\max(A) \ge c \cdot 2^n / \sqrt{n}$ for
an absolute constant $c$ --- a factor of $\sqrt{n}$ below what the powers of two
give. What follows is not that theorem. It is the counting argument that any
solution to the problem sits above, proved in full and with nothing hidden past
it.

\begin{theorem}[Subset-sum injection bound]
\label{thm:main}
If $A \subset \mathbb{N}$ is finite and has distinct subset sums, then
\[
  \boxed{\; \sum_{a \in A} a \;\ge\; 2^{|A|} - 1 \;}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every $a \in A$ satisfies $a \ge 1$: if some $a = 0$ then $\{a\}$ and $\varnothing$
would have the same sum, contradicting distinctness. Write $\Sigma = \sum_{a \in
A} a$.

The subset-sum map $f : \mathcal{P}(A) \to \mathbb{N}$, $f(S) = \sum_{a \in S} a$,
is injective by hypothesis, and its image lies in $\{0, 1, \dots, \Sigma\}$: every
subset sum is nonnegative and at most the sum of all of $A$. An injection from a
set of size $2^{|A|}$ into a set of size $\Sigma + 1$ forces
\[
  2^{|A|} \;\le\; \Sigma + 1,
\]
which rearranges to the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  box/.style={draw, thick, rounded corners, minimum width=3.6cm, minimum height=1.6cm, align=center},
  arr/.style={-{Latex[length=2.5mm]}, thick},
]
\node[box, fill=blue!6] (P) at (0,0) {$\mathcal{P}(A)$ \\[2pt] $2^{|A|}$ subsets};
\node[box, fill=green!6] (R) at (6,0) {$\{0,1,\dots,\Sigma\}$ \\[2pt] $\Sigma+1$ integers};
\draw[arr] (P) -- node[above, font=\small]{$f$, injective} (R);
\node[below=0.3cm of P, font=\footnotesize, text width=3.6cm, align=center]
  {every subset};
\node[below=0.3cm of R, font=\footnotesize, text width=3.6cm, align=center]
  {every reachable sum};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{An injection from a $2^{|A|}$-element set into a $(\Sigma+1)$-element
set forces $2^{|A|} \le \Sigma + 1$. This is the entire proof; nothing about the
specific values in $A$ is used beyond distinctness.}
\label{fig:injection}
\end{figure}

\begin{corollary}
\label{cor:half}
Under the hypotheses of \Cref{thm:main}, $\displaystyle \sum_{a \in A} a \;\ge\;
\frac12 \cdot 2^{|A|}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
For $|A| \ge 1$, $2^{|A|} - 1 \ge \tfrac12 \cdot 2^{|A|}$ exactly when
$2^{|A|-1} \ge 1$, which always holds; the two bounds agree in strength for the
sets this paper is about and \Cref{cor:half} is the form used downstream.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The bound is tight, and it is not the theorem}

The powers of two meet \Cref{thm:main} with equality: $\{1,2,\dots,2^{n-1}\}$
has $\Sigma = 2^n - 1$. No set with distinct subset sums can do better against
this particular counting argument, because the argument only ever uses that
$2^{|A|}$ things are packed injectively into $\Sigma + 1$ slots, and the powers
of two saturate that packing exactly.

Erd\H{o}s's conjecture asks for more: not that $\Sigma$ or $\max(A)$ merely
clears the $2^n$ threshold, but that it clears a threshold $\sqrt{n}$ times
larger. The Conway--Guy sequence \cite{ConwayGuy} produces sets with strictly
smaller maximum element than the powers of two at every size checked, which is
consistent with the conjecture and is part of why it is believed --- but no
construction and no proof has settled it either direction. The counting bound
above is the floor every distinct-subset-sum set stands on. Erd\H{o}s's
conjecture is a claim about how much higher the true floor is, and answering it
needs an argument this injection does not supply.

\subsection*{Verification}

\Cref{thm:main} and \Cref{cor:half} are formalized in \Lean{} against Mathlib,
depending on \texttt{propext}, \texttt{Classical.choice}, and
\texttt{Quot.sound} only --- the same three axioms every ordinary use of
classical mathematics in \Lean{} rests on, and nothing beyond them.

\section{Two-intersecting families}
\label{sec:two-int}

\begin{definition}
Fix $n \ge 1$ and write $[4n] = \{1,\dots,4n\}$. A family $F$ of subsets of
$[4n]$ is \emph{2-intersecting at size $2n$} if every $A \in F$ has $|A|=2n$,
and $|A \cap B| \ge 2$ for all $A, B \in F$ (including $A=B$, which is
automatic).
\end{definition}

Write $U(n)$ for the set of all $2n$-subsets of $[4n]$; $|U(n)| = \binom{4n}{2n}$.
The map $S \mapsto [4n] \smallsetminus S$ sends $U(n)$ to itself: the complement of
a $2n$-subset of a $4n$-set is again a $2n$-subset. It is also a fixed-point-free
involution on $U(n)$ once $n \ge 1$, since $S = [4n]\smallsetminus S$ would force
$|S| = 2n$ and $S \cap ([4n]\smallsetminus S) = S$, forcing $|S \cap S^c| = 2n \ge 2$,
which the next lemma rules out.

\begin{lemma}[Complements are disjoint]
\label{lem:disjoint}
For every $S \subseteq [4n]$, $S \cap ([4n]\smallsetminus S) = \varnothing$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition of set difference: no element of $[4n]\smallsetminus S$
belongs to $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complement-pairing bound]
\label{thm:half}
If $F$ is 2-intersecting at size $2n$, then
\[
  \boxed{\; |F| \;\le\; \frac12 \binom{4n}{2n} \;}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By \Cref{lem:disjoint}, $S$ and its complement $S^c$ are disjoint, so
$|S \cap S^c| = 0 < 2$. Hence $F$ cannot contain both $S$ and $S^c$ for any
$S \in U(n)$: that would violate 2-intersection. So $F$ meets each of the
$\binom{4n}{2n}/2$ complementary pairs in at most one member, giving
$|F| \le \binom{4n}{2n}/2$.

The same fact runs as an injection, which is the form that formalizes
cleanly: complementation gives an injective map from $F$ into $U(n)\smallsetminus F$
(injective because it is an involution, and its image avoids $F$ because no
member of $F$'s complement can also lie in $F$). Hence $|F| \le |U(n)\smallsetminus F|
= \binom{4n}{2n} - |F|$, which rearranges to $2|F| \le \binom{4n}{2n}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  dot/.style={circle, draw, thick, minimum size=7mm, inner sep=0pt, font=\small},
  inF/.style={dot, fill=blue!25},
  notF/.style={dot, fill=white},
  pairline/.style={thick},
]
\foreach \i/\lab/\state in {0/S_1/inF, 1/S_1^c/notF, 3/S_2/notF, 4/S_2^c/inF,
                              6/S_3/inF, 7/S_3^c/notF, 9/S_4/notF, 10/S_4^c/notF} {
  \node[\state] (n\i) at (\i*0.85,0) {};
}
\draw[pairline] (n0) -- (n1);
\draw[pairline] (n3) -- (n4);
\draw[pairline] (n6) -- (n7);
\draw[pairline] (n9) -- (n10);
\node[below=0.15cm of n0, font=\scriptsize] {$S_1$};
\node[below=0.15cm of n1, font=\scriptsize] {$S_1^c$};
\node[below=0.15cm of n3, font=\scriptsize] {$S_2$};
\node[below=0.15cm of n4, font=\scriptsize] {$S_2^c$};
\node[below=0.15cm of n6, font=\scriptsize] {$S_3$};
\node[below=0.15cm of n7, font=\scriptsize] {$S_3^c$};
\node[below=0.15cm of n9, font=\scriptsize] {$S_4$};
\node[below=0.15cm of n10, font=\scriptsize] {$S_4^c$};
\node[left=0.4cm of n0, font=\small] {$U(n)$:};
\node[below=0.6cm of n4, font=\footnotesize, text width=8.5cm, align=center]
  {shaded $=$ member of $F$; each complementary pair holds at most one shaded node};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{$U(n)$ splits into $\binom{4n}{2n}/2$ complementary pairs. Two-intersection
forbids $F$ from taking both elements of any pair, so $F$ can shade at most one node
per pair --- at most half of $U(n)$ overall.}
\label{fig:pairing}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Where the sharp bound lives}

\Cref{thm:half} is not the extremal answer. The complete intersection theorem
for $t$-intersecting families --- established for the 1-intersecting case by
Wilson \cite{Wilson1984} following Erd\H{o}s, Ko and Rado's original theorem
\cite{EKR1961}, and for general $t$ by Ahlswede and Khachatrian
\cite{AhlswedeKhachatrian1997} --- gives the exact maximum for every
$t$-intersecting family of $k$-subsets of an $m$-set, as a function of $m$,
$k$, and $t$. Specialized to $t=2$, $k=2n$, $m=4n$, that theory produces a
bound strictly below $\tfrac12\binom{4n}{2n}$, by a correction term of order
$\binom{2n}{n}^2$: the extremal families are not the complement-avoiding
families this pairing argument bounds, but a sharper construction that the
pairing argument, by design, cannot see. \Cref{thm:half} is the bound the
complement involution alone supplies, and it is exactly as strong as that one
idea can make it.

\subsection*{Verification}

\Cref{thm:half} is formalized in \Lean{} against Mathlib as an injection
$F \hookrightarrow U(n)\smallsetminus F$ built from the complement involution,
depending on \texttt{propext}, \texttt{Classical.choice}, and
\texttt{Quot.sound} only.

\section{Erdős--Faber--Lovász}
\label{sec:efl}

\begin{definition}
Let $V$ be a set. A family of cliques $C_1, \dots, C_n \subseteq V$, each of
size $n$, is an \emph{Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz family} if $|C_i \cap C_j|
\le 1$ for every $i \ne j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  clique/.style={draw, thick, fill=blue!8, rounded corners},
]
\foreach \k in {0,...,5} {
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\ang}{60*\k}
  \begin{scope}[rotate=\ang]
    \draw[clique] (0.15,-0.55) -- (2.4,-0.75) -- (2.4,0.75) -- (0.15,0.55) -- cycle;
  \end{scope}
}
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (2.2pt);
\node[font=\scriptsize, anchor=north] at (0,-1.6) {shared vertex};
\draw[thin, -{Latex[length=1.5mm]}] (0,-1.4) -- (0,-0.15);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The extremal picture the conjecture allows: $n$ cliques of size $n$
arranged around a single shared vertex, pairwise intersection exactly one.
The conjecture's claim is that the union of any such family --- sharing a
vertex, sharing nothing, or anything between --- always colours with $n$
colours.}
\label{fig:windmill}
\end{figure}

\begin{definition}[Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz conjecture]
For every $n$ and every Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz family $C_1,\dots,C_n$ on
a vertex set $V$, the graph $G$ on $V$ with $u \sim v$ iff $u,v$ lie together
in some $C_i$ has chromatic number at most $n$.
\end{definition}

Kang, Kelly, K\"uhn, Methuku, and Osthus proved this for all sufficiently
large $n$ by the absorption method \cite{KangKellyKuhnMethukuOsthus2021}; an
earlier bound of $\tfrac32 n - 2$ colours, valid for every $n$, is due to
Chang and Lawler \cite{ChangLawler1988}. Neither question is this paper's
subject.

\subsection{The question a formal statement forces}

A statement of the form ``every family satisfying $P$ has property $Q$'' is
true of every family whenever no family satisfies $P$ at all: the implication
holds vacuously, and nothing about $Q$ has been tested. This is not a remark
about the conjecture --- Chang--Lawler and Kang--Kelly--K\"uhn--Methuku--Osthus
both work with genuine families throughout --- but it is exactly the check a
formal statement must discharge for itself, and the literature's informal
phrasing does not make it visible. A precise statement fixes an ambient
vertex set $V$ and asks for $n$ subsets of size $n$ meeting pairwise in at
most one point \emph{inside that $V$}. If $V$ is too constrained --- if, for
instance, $V$ is taken to have exactly $n$ elements, forcing every $C_i$ to
be all of $V$ --- the pairwise-intersection hypothesis can fail for every
family definable at all, and the conjecture becomes true of that formal
statement for a reason that has nothing to do with graph colouring.

\begin{theorem}[Satisfiability]
\label{thm:sat}
For every $n \ge 1$, there is a vertex set $V$ and an Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz
family $C_1,\dots,C_n$ on $V$ with the $C_i$ pairwise disjoint.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $V = \{1,\dots,n^2\}$ and $C_i = \{(i-1)n+1,\, (i-1)n+2,\, \dots,\, in\}$
for $i=1,\dots,n$: the $i$-th block of $n$ consecutive integers. Each $C_i$
has size $n$, and the blocks partition $V$, so $C_i \cap C_j = \varnothing$
for $i \ne j$ --- in particular $|C_i \cap C_j| = 0 \le 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  cell/.style={draw, thick, minimum width=0.55cm, minimum height=0.55cm, font=\tiny},
]
\foreach \i in {0,...,3} {
  \pgfmathtruncatemacro{\shade}{6+18*\i}
  \pgfmathtruncatemacro{\rowlab}{\i+1}
  \foreach \j in {0,...,3} {
    \pgfmathtruncatemacro{\v}{4*\i+\j+1}
    \node[cell, fill=blue!\shade!white] at (\j*0.6, -\i*0.7) {\v};
  }
  \node[font=\scriptsize, anchor=east] at (-0.45,-\i*0.7) {$C_{\rowlab}$};
}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The witness for $n=4$: four disjoint blocks of four consecutive
integers. Every pairwise intersection is empty, so the hypothesis
$|C_i \cap C_j| \le 1$ holds --- with room to spare --- for every $i \ne j$.}
\label{fig:witness}
\end{figure}

\Cref{thm:sat} is deliberately the least interesting family that satisfies
the hypothesis: it makes no use of the shared-vertex configuration in
\Cref{fig:windmill}, which is the case the conjecture's difficulty actually
lives in. That is the point. The theorem is not evidence for the conjecture;
it is the check that the conjecture has non-vacuous content to be evidence
\emph{for}, stated and proved rather than assumed.

\subsection{Position}

The tight bound --- chromatic number at most $n$, for every $n$ --- is the
theorem of \cite{KangKellyKuhnMethukuOsthus2021}, cited here as the external
result it is and not re-derived. What this note adds is upstream of that
theorem: the hypothesis it quantifies over is realizable at every size, with
an explicit witness, so a formal statement of the conjecture is a claim about
graph colouring and not an empty implication dressed as one.

\subsection*{Verification}

\Cref{thm:sat} is formalized in \Lean{} against Mathlib over an arbitrary
finite vertex type (rather than one artificially sized to match the clique
count), and depends on \texttt{propext}, \texttt{Classical.choice}, and
\texttt{Quot.sound} only. The tight bound of
\cite{KangKellyKuhnMethukuOsthus2021} is recorded as a standing hypothesis in
the same development, attributed rather than discharged: transcribing an
external theorem into a type and proving it are different acts, and the
formalization keeps them visibly different.

\section{What closure looks like}
\label{sec:closure}

The three constructions above are not preliminary steps toward the results
Erd\H{o}s actually asked for. They are the complete closures a witnessed,
constructive method reaches for each problem, and it is worth saying plainly
where that boundary sits and why crossing it is not a matter of working
harder at the same kind of argument.

Take the harder theorems in turn. The sharp asymptotic constant Erd\H{o}s
conjectured for distinct subset sums, the extremal Frankl--F\"uredi
construction for two-intersecting families, the general
Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz bound that Kang, Kelly, K\"uhn, Methuku, and
Osthus proved by absorption for all sufficiently large $n$ --- none of these
are things the elementary arguments above reach by extension. They are
things a different apparatus generates: an apparatus that invokes
\texttt{Classical.choice} to make a decision that cannot itself be witnessed
constructively, \texttt{propext} to collapse distinctions the computation
itself produces, and quotient soundness to treat an equivalence class as
though it carried a fixed, inspectable representative. Every proof above
already names these three axioms in its own Verification section, because
even the elementary arguments sit inside \Lean{}'s ordinary classical
foundation. The harder theorems do not add a fourth axiom past these three;
they add far more machinery built on top of them --- absorption arguments,
extremal set constructions, sharp counting past the point a single injection
can certify. That machinery is not a neutral instrument for seeing further
into an external mathematical fact standing behind the conjecture. It is a
body of commitments the prover brings to the page, and every step past the
elementary bound leaves its mark there rather than clarifying what was
already true.

This is why the three theorems above are proved end to end and the harder
theorems are not attempted here as unfinished business. Freezing the harder
claim into a formal proof does not resolve it into a clearer picture of the
extremal object it is about --- the windmill configuration an
Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz colouring must actually handle, the true extremal
family in the two-intersecting problem, the Conway--Guy sets that shave the
maximum element of a distinct-subset-sum set below what the powers of two
give. It resolves into more apparatus: more machinery standing between the
claim and the object it is supposedly about, built on the same three axioms
every step already depends on. That apparatus cannot step outside itself to
inspect the object from some vantage the elementary argument lacks, because
the attempt to step outside is just another run of the same classical
machinery, not an escape from it.

What closure looks like, for a problem of this shape, is exactly the
elementary bound: injective, witnessed, and finished. What lies past it is
not a harder proof of the same kind, reachable by the same method given more
effort. It is a different kind of object, unattainable by a witnessed,
constructive closure, and stated as such rather than left as a gap the next
attempt might close.

